  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Two convex lenses of focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> each are placed coaxially with a separation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>&nbsp;between them. The image of the distant object formed by the combination is at _____ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></math>&nbsp;from the first lens.</p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "100",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Let&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;be the first lens and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;be the second lens.</p><p>The first refraction is in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>.</p><p>Using lens formula,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>&#8734;</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p><p>Hence, the distance between the second image and the&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;would be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>.</p><p>For the second lens&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>40</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>40</mn></mfrac><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p><p>So, the distance of image from first lens is</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p>"
  }
}